197:## Exclude content from a prefetch

When the lookup is tied to a single session, use [`"use cache: private"`](/docs/app/api-reference/directives/use-cache-private). It assigns a cache lifetime to a function that reads cookies, headers, or other runtime data directly. Results are cached in the browser only, scoped to that session.

```tsx filename="app/dashboard/user-nav.tsx"
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

async function UserNav() {
  const user = await getUser()
  return <nav>{user.name}</nav>
}

async function getUser() {
  'use cache: private'
  const session = (await cookies()).get('session')?.value
  return db.users.findBySession(session)
}
```

Here `cookies()` lives inside the cached function, which only works under `"use cache: private"`. This is also the pattern when you can't extract the runtime data from the outside: auth helpers that check `Date.now()` against a token's expiry, or session helpers that read cookies deep inside their own code, can't be wrapped at the call site.

Everything inside the scope shares the same lifetime. Colocate `"use cache: private"` as close to the runtime data access as possible.

## Exclude content from a prefetch

Optimizing a prefetch also means excluding content from it. Some subtrees are expensive enough to produce that you would rather not pay for them on every prefetch of the route.

Awaiting [`unstable_navigation()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/navigation) above the work leaves it out of every prefetch, so it is produced on the navigation. The nearest [`<Suspense>`](https://react.dev/reference/react/Suspense) fallback is what the user sees while it streams in. Take the dashboard again, where a ranking keyed on the session is too expensive to produce for every link into the route:

```tsx filename="app/dashboard/recommendations.tsx" highlight={6}
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'
import { unstable_navigation } from 'next/cache'

async function Recommendations() {
  const session = (await cookies()).get('session')?.value
  await unstable_navigation()
  const items = await rank(session)
  return <List items={items} />
}

async function rank(session?: string) {
  'use cache: private'
  return db.recommendations.rank({ session })
}
```

The session is read above `unstable_navigation()`, so it resolves in the shell like any other session data. Only the ranking is left out, and it keeps its cache lifetime.

Without it, keeping a subtree out of a prefetch means leaving it uncached, which gives up that lifetime on the navigation too. Awaiting `unstable_navigation()` inside a cached scope is not allowed, so put the caching directive on a function called below it, as `rank` does here.

For a subtree that would otherwise sit in the App Shell, but that a `<Link prefetch={true}>` should still resolve before the click, use [`unstable_prefetch()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/prefetch) instead.

Unlike [`connection()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/connection), `unstable_navigation()` does not make the subtree request-dependent. This lets reusable content keep its cache lifetime while waiting for navigation. See [How `unstable_navigation()` differs from `connection()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/navigation#how-unstable_navigation-differs-from-connection) for the full comparison.

## Test prefetched and deferred content

The [Instant navigation guide](/docs/app/guides/instant-navigation#prevent-regressions-with-e2e-tests) explains how to install and configure `@next/playwright`. For a client navigation, use `instant()` to inspect the destination's prefetched UI.

When [adopting Partial Prefetching](/docs/app/guides/adopting-partial-prefetching#verify-prefetched-ui-with-tests), run this test before enabling the feature and rerun the same assertions afterward. This preserves the UI selected from the legacy full prefetch while its implementation moves to the App Shell or a per-link prefetch.

Inside the callback, click the link, wait for the destination URL, and assert which content is available. After the callback releases the navigation, assert that deferred content eventually renders:

```ts filename="e2e/product-navigation.test.ts" highlight={8-15,17}
import { expect, test } from '@playwright/test'
import { instant } from '@next/playwright'

test('prefetches the product summary but not reviews', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/products')

  await instant(page, async () => {
    await page.click('a[href="/products/baseball-cap"]')
    await page.waitForURL((url) => url.pathname === '/products/baseball-cap')

    await expect(
      page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Baseball Cap' })
    ).toBeVisible()
    await expect(page.getByTestId('product-summary')).toBeVisible()
    await expect(page.getByTestId('reviews')).toHaveCount(0)
  })

  await expect(page.getByTestId('reviews')).toBeVisible()
})

